---
/**
 * LoanAndPlacement — the two amounts that sit outside the package totals,
 * /pricing only.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SECTION EXISTS TO CORRECT. The site used to say that further
 * placement-related fees "may apply", which a reader takes to mean a cost
 * the employer bears, possibly, on top of the package. Both halves of that
 * are wrong. Core-pages design spec §2.4, binding on all copy: the
 * employer ADVANCES the loan and the placement fee and RECOVERS them
 * through the helper's repayment. It is ultimately the HELPER'S cost. Copy
 * must state both halves — the money leaves the employer's account first,
 * and it comes back — because either half alone is a different and untrue
 * claim.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND MUST STAY ABSENT.
 *
 * DirectHired supplied the repayment mechanics on 2026-08-16: the range it
 * runs over, where in the helper's pay it comes from, and what she
 * receives alongside it. None of that is published here, on any other
 * page, or in any FAQ answer. It describes a salary-deduction arrangement
 * on a licensed employment agency's public website, in an area MOM
 * regulates closely, and source brief §19 requires the final wording to be
 * reviewed against DirectHired's actual commercial and regulatory terms
 * before it goes out. It is the highest-liability sentence on the site.
 *
 * The gap is recorded as a declared input in
 * scripts/generate-info-required.mjs (Category C of
 * docs/INFORMATION-REQUIRED-BEFORE-PRODUCTION.md) and enforced by
 * tests/compliance-gate.test.ts, which scans the built HTML. What is
 * missing is sign-off, not information.
 *
 * A PARAPHRASE IS A BREACH. Writing "over several months", "from what she
 * earns", or "she keeps her rest-day pay" would publish the same
 * arrangement in different words and would pass a literal search for the
 * supplied phrases. Do not gesture at the mechanism. The page is complete
 * and honest without it: a family needs to know who ultimately bears the
 * cost, which is stated here in full.
 *
 * The loan carries no figure and no range — spec §2.3 says it is genuinely
 * case-by-case and §7 forbids publishing a range. "It depends" is the
 * true answer, so it is given as the answer rather than softened into an
 * approximate one.
 */
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro'
import Card from '../components/Card.astro'
import { company } from '../data/company'
---

<section class="loan-placement">
  <Container>
    {/*
      "They are the ones most often misunderstood" is gone. It was a claim
      about what DirectHired's customers misunderstand and no source
      supplies it — the same unverifiable-behaviour class as the FAQ lede on
      /pricing, and as the claim already cut from an FAQ entry. What is left
      says only what the section does, which needs no authority.
    */}
    <SectionHeader
      title="The loan and the placement fee"
      lede="Two amounts sit outside the package totals above. Here is what they are and who ends up paying them."
    />

    <div class="loan-grid">
      <Card>
        <div class="amount-card">
          <h3>Placement fee</h3>
          <p class="amount-figure">One month’s salary</p>
          <p>
            Fixed at one month’s salary, and the same amount whether you take on a new helper or a
            transfer helper. There is no separate scale to work through.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Card>

      <Card>
        <div class="amount-card">
          <h3>Helper loan</h3>
          <p class="amount-figure">Assessed case by case</p>
          <p>
            The loan depends on the individual helper, so there is no standard figure to publish —
            and we would rather publish none than one that turns out not to be yours.
            {' '}
            {/*
              The sentence that offers to tell you the number was the one
              sentence on this page that offered no way to ask for it. It is
              now the link, pointed at the same single-sourced destination as
              every primary CTA on the site — company.requirementFormUrl,
              never a literal (tests/links.test.ts).

              "…BEFORE YOU COMMIT TO ANYTHING" IS DELETED, and must not come
              back. It is an unauthorised process promise — zero hits in the
              master brief — and commit ff4d631 formally removed that exact
              phrase from src/content/faq/helper-loan-placement-fee.md on
              those grounds. Commit be9dfea had already copied it into this
              file, so the ruling took it out of the FAQ while it survived
              here, and the /pricing CTA work then attached it to the
              requirement-form link: the promise ended up sitting on the
              conversion path, which is the worst place for it. Its entry in
              COPY_THAT_MUST_NOT_BE_CAUGHT (tests/compliance-gate.test.ts)
              went with it — that list was asserting it as approved copy.

              For anyone reading the live audit's C4, which proposes asking
              DirectHired to approve a sentence containing "before you
              commit": that request was answered — spec §2.6.2 approves
              "We go through the repayment arrangement in full with you
              before you commit, and it is set out in the contract", and it
              ships further down this file, in the paragraph about who bears
              the cost. It is not this link, and it must not become this
              link.

              AND NEITHER WAS THE REST OF THE SENTENCE (2026-08-17). The
              anchor text read "Our team goes through the exact amount for
              the helper you choose". The comment above it said in terms that
              this phrasing "was never approved", and the §2.6.2 note further
              down said the approved sentence is "deliberately NOT attached
              to the requirement-form link in the card above… the worst place
              for an unapproved promise". The file documented the defect and
              then shipped it: an unapproved promise as the anchor text of a
              primary CTA, on the conversion path, in a card whose whole
              subject is a figure §2.3 forbids publishing.

              What replaced it promises nothing that is not already
              authorised. "Submit your requirements" is the site's own CTA
              label (Hero.astro, MobileCtaBar.astro, FinalCta) and names the
              destination rather than an outcome. "We will come back to you"
              is already published on /faq and /find-your-helper, and rests
              on the approved response answer in
              src/content/faq/response-time.md ("We aim to respond within 1
              business day…"). Neither half says a figure will be produced,
              which is the promise §2.3 cannot support and the old anchor
              made.
            */}
            <a href={company.requirementFormUrl}>Submit your requirements</a> and we will come back
            to you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Card>
    </div>

    {/*
      THE PARAGRAPH THE PAGE EXISTS TO DELIVER, given the weight to match.

      The copy here is unchanged and was never the problem: it states both
      halves of spec §2.4 in balance, which is exactly what is required. The
      defect was that it carried less visual weight than "Handling &
      transport $120.00" — a 24px heading and one 16px paragraph on bare
      cream, no panel, no rule, no tint, sitting immediately after two
      raised white cards that take all the attention, at precisely the point
      a reader is deciding whether they have been dealt with straight.

      The treatment is the one already in the system: white on the cream
      ground, the inverse of the carry-forward panel's raised surface on
      /pricing. No new colour and no new fact.
    */}
    <div class="who-pays">
      <h3 class="who-pays-heading">Who actually bears these</h3>
      <p class="who-pays-lede">
        You advance both amounts at the start, and you recover them through the helper’s repayment.
        Both halves matter: the money does leave your account first, and it does come back.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are ultimately the <strong>helper’s</strong> cost, not an additional charge you carry
        on top of the package.
      </p>
      {/*
        APPROVED VERBATIM BY DIRECTHIRED, 2026-08-16. Core-pages design spec
        §2.6.2. Do not paraphrase it, and do not extend it.

        IT IS ABOUT PROCESS, NOT MECHANISM, and that is precisely why it
        clears §2.5's gate. It tells a reader that the arrangement is
        explained to them and written into the contract. It discloses
        nothing about how the arrangement works: not how long repayment
        runs, not where in the helper's pay it comes from, not what she
        receives during it. All three remain gated pending compliance
        sign-off, in substance as well as literally — see this file's
        header, which is unchanged and still the real rule. One sentence was
        authorised. It is not a foothold for the mechanics.

        "BEFORE YOU COMMIT" — AND THIS IS NOT A CONTRADICTION. The clause
        "…before you commit to anything" was struck from this file and from
        src/content/faq/helper-loan-placement-fee.md (ff4d631) as an
        unauthorised process promise, and its entry was struck from
        COPY_THAT_MUST_NOT_BE_CAUGHT for asserting it as approved copy. That
        was correct: nothing had approved it. The live audit's C4 proposed
        asking DirectHired to approve a sentence containing the idea, and
        this is the answer to that request — the same idea, now authorised,
        in DirectHired's own words rather than ours. The deletion stands for
        the old clause; this sentence exists because it was asked for.

        It is deliberately NOT attached to the requirement-form link in the
        card above, which is where the old clause ended up and the worst
        place for an unapproved promise. It sits here, in the paragraph
        about who bears the cost, which is what it qualifies.

        The gate's patterns were narrowed by exactly one token to admit it —
        "arrangement" left the duration alternation in
        tests/compliance-gate.test.ts, where the reasoning is recorded — and
        the sentence is now a permanent fixture there so no future pattern
        can start eating approved copy.
      */}
      <p>
        We go through the repayment arrangement in full with you before you commit, and it is set
        out in the contract.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  .loan-placement {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* Cream, so the two Cards below read as raised against it — the same
       reasoning PricingSection.astro records for block 06. Also puts this
       section between the white replacement block above and the white FAQ
       below, keeping the page's grounds alternating. */
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .loan-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .amount-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .amount-card h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .amount-figure {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    /* 1.75rem, matching PricingCard's `.total-amount` exactly — the size
       every package total on this page is set at. "One month's salary"
       and "assessed case by case" ARE the answers to "how much?", and
       setting them as prose while every real number on the page is set as
       a figure would read as evasion.

       Precisely: `.total-amount` is a flat 1.75rem at every viewport
       (PricingCard declares no media queries at all). Its 2.25rem rule is
       `.total-only .total-amount` — a VARIANT for a package with no line
       items, which has had zero instances since 2026-08-16, not a
       breakpoint step. So the two match as rendered today. If a
       total-only package ever ships, these figures sit beside a 2.25rem
       total and this comment is where to decide whether to follow it;
       the answer is probably no, since the step exists to stop a card
       with nothing else on it reading as sparse, which is not this
       card's problem. */
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .amount-card p:not(.amount-figure) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* --color-accent (6.03:1 on the cream card), never --color-brand-teal,
     which measures 2.89:1 and carries no text anywhere on this site. Same
     treatment as PricingSection's qualifier link on the homepage. */
  .amount-card a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .amount-card a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .who-pays {
    /* The measure stays 60ch; the panel is what is new. Its own padding is
       outside that, so the line length a reader gets is unchanged. */
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
    padding: var(--space-8);
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }

  .who-pays-heading {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .who-pays p {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* The two-clause sentence — "the money does leave your account first, and
     it does come back" — is the one a reader must not misread, so it is set
     a step above the paragraph that follows it. */
  .who-pays .who-pays-lede {
    /* Two class selectors, not one: `.who-pays p` above is (0,1,1) and
       would otherwise win the font-size on specificity alone. */
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* 48em = --bp-tablet (tokens.css); the literal is repeated by hand
     because custom properties do not resolve inside a media condition. */
  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    .loan-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      /*
       * `start`, not `stretch`. These two cards are not a like-for-like
       * comparison the way the two package cards are — one answers "how
       * much" with a fixed rule, the other answers it with "assessed case
       * by case" and a paragraph explaining why — so their content lengths
       * legitimately differ. Stretching matched the shorter card to the
       * taller and left ~77px of empty white at the foot of the placement
       * fee card at 1280, which reads as a missing sentence rather than as
       * a card that is simply shorter.
       *
       * The package grids keep `stretch` for the opposite reason: there the
       * two cards ARE the comparison, and unequal heights would read as
       * unequal offers.
       */
      align-items: start;
    }
  }
</style>
